  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Georgian Trade Unions Confederation (GTUC) is the main trade union in Georgia. It unites 21 sectoral trade unions and has around 150,000 members. The GTUC is affiliated to the International Trade Union Confederation (ITUC) and Pan-European Regional Council (PERC) since 2006. Difficult conditions for collective bargaining in various sectors remain one of the main challenges for the GTUC if it is to enhance working conditions for current and future members. In developing and implementing national labour policies, including wages, occupational health and safety and informality, the voice of workers is essential for the promotion of human-centred policies grounded in social justice. For the GTUC, the EU accession process can be a tool to strengthen social dialogue, increase access to workers&#x2019; rights and enhance working conditions through collective bargaining.  

The ILO supports trade unions to conduct research and develop evidence-based policy positions on just transition, wage policies and addressing the gender pay gap and informality.  
On just transition, the ILO equips GTUC with knowledge on the principles underpinning the concept of just transition, the Paris Agreement and the NDCs and possible trade union avenues to influence national processes. The work relies on ACTRAV&#x2019;s User&#x2019;s manual to the ILO&#x2019;s Guidelines for a Just Transition Towards Environmentally Sustainable Economies and Societies for All.  
Further, the ILO provides training to GTUC for enhanced advocacy and communication skills to promote their position papers.</narrative>
  </description>
